{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_u7abobbh6fcmwrbwvprsgkm3irnxzzv5bagjgv6467zakuy4tojq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass, independently re-verified.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 63-line file exporting three symbols: `fma_array` (elementwise `out[i] = mul1[i]*mul2[i] + add[i]`), `call_fma` (builds `ones`/`zeros` VLAs, returns `out[len-1]`), and `driver` (an `sscanf(\"%d%zn\")` parse loop capped at 100 ints → `call_fma` → `printf`). Note `driver.h` declares only `driver()`, but the other two are non-`static` and so part of the ABI — both are driven **directly**, not just through the wrapper.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| gate | status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — symbol diff empty, 0 undefined non-libc symbols | **PASS** (3/3, nothing untranslated) |\n| Phase B — every `CONFIGS.md` row green over randomized inputs | **PASS** (34 rows ↔ 34 tests) |\n| Phase C — every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing differential test | **PASS** (21 rows + 2 generic-boundary rows ↔ 23 tests) |\n| Every feature combination | **PASS** (no `[features]`, so `{--no-default-features, default} × {release, dev}` = 4, all green) |\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed in the Rust\n\n1. **Debug-profile pointer UB trapped differently.** `fma_array` used `*mul1.offset(i)`. Under `-C debug-assertions=on` rustc inserts a null-pointer check on raw-pointer place expressions, so a `NULL` argument **aborted with SIGABRT where C faults with SIGSEGV**. Fixed with `wrapping_offset` + `core::ptr::read`/`write`, which lower to the same instructions and carry no checks. This only surfaced because Phase D runs the *dev* profile too — the default-only run was green.\n2. **`sscanf` entry point.** The C object links `__isoc99_sscanf`; Rust was binding glibc's legacy `sscanf`, a genuinely different function. Now bound explicitly.\n\n## Two documented, deliberate deviations\n\nBoth are cases where C produces no *result* to match, so each is pinned down by a test rather than glossed over: negative `len` (VLA of negative size — C returns run-to-run garbage: measured `-991075438`, then `32767`), and `len` past the stack budget (C works at 690 000, faults at 700 000; Rust heap-allocates). `err04`/`err21` assert what is assertable and confirm the C side really is the UB case.\n\n## Why I trust the green suite\n\nPassing tests prove nothing unless they can fail, so I added `scripts/mutation_check.sh`: 32 injected bugs, 28 must-detect (**all detected**) and 4 provably-equivalent (all correctly survived). It exposed a real coverage hole — nothing tested a `NULL` **output** pointer, where the loads succeed and the *store* faults — which I then closed.\n\nTwo harness traps worth flagging, since both initially produced misleading results: `libtest` writes its own `test ... ok` progress lines to fd 1, which spliced into in-process stdout captures and manufactured fake divergences (now captured in a forked child); and `cargo test` does not build a `cdylib`-only lib target, so the harness was silently loading the *release* `.so` while running the *dev* test binary — it now hard-fails instead."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":884,"cache_read_input_tokens":220173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":884,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1fb958d-d1d5-4563-a891-35147c9ad97a","uuid":"63e25925-67ce-4f19-af92-cce9a67d02cb","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:51:34.924Z"}
